// Governing: ADR-0005 (Scrape Item Stats into a Generated, Committed Data File)
// Implements: SPEC-0007 REQ "Generated, Committed Stats File"
//
// The item-stats seam, mirroring what `hunters.js` is for the roster.
//
// `itemStats.json` is GENERATED by `node scripts/scrape-stats.mjs` and committed — never
// hand-edited. Re-running the scrape rewrites it in place. Everything downstream reads through
// here, so when a stat display lands it needs no knowledge of the file's shape.
//
// The file is `{ _generated: {...}, items: { [catalogId]: record } }`. The wrapper exists because
// JSON carries no comments and SPEC-0007 requires the generated-ness to be visible to whoever
// opens the file; unwrapping it here means no consumer has to know that.
//
// `statsFor` returns null for an unknown id, and that is the specified behaviour rather than a
// gap. SPEC-0007 requires the app to degrade cleanly for an item with no scraped stats — the same
// posture ItemThumb takes when no image exists. The dataset and the catalog refresh independently:
// a newly added catalog row and an item whose page failed to parse are the same state to a
// consumer, and both resolve to null here.

import data from "./itemStats.json";

/** The generated payload's provenance header. */
export const STATS_GENERATED = data._generated ?? null;

/** Every scraped record, keyed by catalog id. */
export const ITEM_STATS = data.items ?? {};

/**
 * The scraped record for a catalog id, or null when there is none.
 *
 * Callers MUST tolerate null rather than assuming coverage — the scrape can fail one item without
 * failing the run, so a partially covered dataset is a normal state, not a broken one.
 */
export function statsFor(id) {
  if (!id) return null;
  return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(ITEM_STATS, id) ? ITEM_STATS[id] : null;
}

/**
 * Whether the wiki says this weapon can be dual wielded — `true`, `false`, or `null` for not stated.
 *
 * Governing: #178. Added with the scrape's `dualWield` field.
 *
 * THREE-VALUED, and a caller must not collapse it. `true` is read from the page's own words; `false`
 * means a description was read and did not say it, which is an inference from ABSENCE because the wiki
 * asserts dual-wieldability positively and never denies it; `null` means nothing was read.
 *
 * That matters because #178 notes the attribute "feeds the budget math the same way `size` and `cost`
 * do" — a dual-wielded pair costs more slots than one pistol. So `if (dualWieldFor(id))` is safe for
 * offering an affordance, and `if (dualWieldFor(id) === false)` is NOT safe as proof a weapon cannot
 * be paired. Consuming this in the builder is #179, and it needs the catalog schema work first.
 */
export function dualWieldFor(id) {
  const value = statsFor(id)?.dualWield;
  return value === undefined ? null : value;
}

/**
 * The scraped description for a catalog id, or null.
 *
 * Governing: ADR-0005 (the "and Descriptions" half of its title), SPEC-0007 REQ "Generated,
 * Committed Stats File". Added with #228.
 *
 * Read through here rather than off the record so callers do not have to know that a description can
 * be absent, or that its paragraphs are newline-joined. Null is a normal outcome: a page can carry a
 * hatnote and no prose, and a catalog row can predate the dataset — those are the same state to a
 * caller, exactly as `statsFor` already documents.
 *
 * The value is UNTRUSTED wiki text. SPEC-0003 states this rule for the hunter descriptions and it
 * applies identically here: render it as text, never as markup. The scrape strips tags, which makes
 * the stored value text rather than a fragment; it does not make it safe to inject.
 */
export function descriptionFor(id) {
  const value = statsFor(id)?.description;
  return value === undefined || value === "" ? null : value;
}

/**
 * One infobox field for a catalog id, or null.
 *
 * Values are the wiki's own strings ("75", "Compact"), deliberately not coerced to numbers here:
 * ADR-0005 gates numeric write-through behind range assertions in the scrape, and a silent
 * `Number()` at the read end would reintroduce exactly the unvalidated coercion those assertions
 * exist to prevent.
 */
export function statFieldFor(id, field) {
  const record = statsFor(id);
  if (!record?.fields) return null;
  const value = record.fields[field];
  return value === undefined || value === "" ? null : value;
}
